use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Disclosure import Disclosure
from ../../../behaviors/Dismissal import Dismissal
from ../../../behaviors/Positioning import Positioning
from ../../../behaviors/Presence import Presence
from ../../Link import Link

export ui-component NavigationMenu:
    description:
        A navigation menu is a site's main set of links, usually a bar across
        the top. Some of its items are links; others open a panel of further
        links and content, such as "Products" opening a panel listing every
        product.

    composes:
        - @{Link}

    behaviors:
        - @{Disclosure}
        - @{Positioning}
        - @{Dismissal}
        - @{Presence}

    items:
        Each top-level item is either a Link that goes somewhere, or a trigger
        that opens a panel. A trigger only opens its panel; it never goes
        anywhere itself. The whole menu has a name, such as "Main", so
        assistive tools can list it with other navigation on the page.

    opening:
        Clicking or pressing a trigger opens its panel, and doing it again
        closes it. Resting the pointer on a trigger also opens it after a short
        delay, and moving away closes it after another, so the pointer can
        cross the gap into the panel without it closing. The component can
        change these delays, or turn off opening on hover.

    oneAtATime:
        Only one panel is open at a time. While one is open, moving to another
        trigger switches straight to its panel, without waiting.

    keyboard:
        Every top-level item is its own stop in the Tab order. The arrow keys
        along the bar also move between them, and Home and End go to the first
        and last. From a trigger whose panel is open, Tab moves into the panel,
        and on out of it to the next top-level item. Inside a panel, the arrow
        keys move between its links. Which keys open a panel is up to the
        chosen library.

    closing:
        A panel closes the ways Dismissal describes. Escape closes it and puts
        focus back on its trigger. Following a link inside it closes it too,
        unless the component says otherwise.

    current:
        The link to the page the user is on is marked as the current page,
        both on screen and for assistive tools. A trigger whose panel holds
        the current page can be marked too.

    panels:
        Each panel can sit under its own trigger, or all panels can share one
        area under the whole bar that changes size smoothly as the user moves
        between triggers. Which is up to the chosen library. A panel can hold
        a further level of navigation of its own.

    orientation:
        The menu usually runs across the top of the page, with the arrow keys
        reversed for right-to-left languages. It can also run down the side.

    disabled:
        A disabled trigger can't open its panel, and the arrow keys pass over
        it.

    nonGoals:
        - Not a menu of actions, like an app's File and Edit menus. It holds
            links to places
        - Doesn't block the rest of the page or keep focus inside a panel
        - No small-screen version. Folding it into a single menu button on a
            narrow screen is up to the component using it
